{
  "domain": "internet-number-registry-and-routing-trust",
  "title": "Competition and Adjacent Player Landscape for Internet Number Registry and Routing Trust",
  "generated_on": "2026-05-01",
  "scope": {
    "description": "The landscape includes peer Regional Internet Registries that shape global number-resource policy and operating norms, plus adjacent commercial infrastructure providers that influence DNS, DDoS protection, CDN, IP address leasing, routing trust, and Internet operations tooling expectations.",
    "inclusion_logic": [
      "peer RIRs with responsibility for Internet number resources outside the RIPE NCC service region",
      "commercial DNS registry, managed DNS, CDN, DDoS, and edge-cloud providers that shape operational trust and availability expectations for Internet infrastructure teams",
      "commercial IP address management and leasing marketplaces that affect how organizations think about IPv4 scarcity, transfers, address quality, geolocation, and reputation"
    ],
    "caveats": [
      "RIRs are regional stewardship organizations rather than direct commercial competitors; they are included because they materially shape policy, member experience, registry accuracy, RPKI practice, and global coordination.",
      "Commercial metrics are not directly comparable to RIR metrics. Revenue, domain-name base, customer count, security revenue, or IP addresses under management are preserved exactly at the source-reported scope.",
      "DDoS-Guard is included because it is a visible commercial infrastructure-services player named in the prompt, but no official, current operating metrics suitable for this model were found during research, so its business_stats list is intentionally empty."
    ],
    "notes": [
      "Business stats use official company, investor relations, annual report, member update, financial, or official statistics pages.",
      "RIPE NCC is included as the focal player so its scale, service scope, and charging model can be compared with peer RIRs and adjacent infrastructure players without treating all players as direct commercial competitors."
    ]
  },
